Subject: Cantor Attention Engineering Intervention Layers

Description: A precise boundary for prompt extension, shared inference, learned routing, direct activation intervention, and the staged hybrid architecture through which Cantor shapes attention without confusing neural proposals with semantic authority.

& [AttentionEngineeringIdentity] is the disciplined design of information, control, retrieval, review, and optional model interventions that shape what an inference process can notice, retain, relate, challenge, and act upon
  + [specification_uuid] is b1ae0b1b-8c13-4667-b276-8b94c92f506b
  + [external_form] is signed frames, retrieved evidence, attention directives, tool calls, faculty passes, checkpoints, and review
  + [internal_form] is optional learned prefixes, adapters, activation steering, attention masks, cache control, or model-architecture changes
  = must: name whether an intervention changes model inputs, orchestration state, learned parameters, or hidden activations
  - never: claim that text directives directly set transformer attention weights

& [InterventionLayerSet] is the non-collapsed placement of Cantor controls
  + [layer_1_source_and_compile] is parse signed SOP, establish semantic identity, construct typed relations, compile exact indexes, and bind digests before any model call
  + [layer_2_training_and_adaptation] is train tool-selection behavior, routers, rerankers, adapters, learned prefixes, or other model-specific control artifacts from reviewed examples
  + [layer_3_pre_inference] is resolve purpose, terms, authority, constraints, artifacts, faculties, and dependency closure into an immutable ready frame
  + [layer_4_inter_pass_runtime] is call tools, retrieve additional SOP, exchange addressed observations, compare faculty outputs, and reframe through multiple checkpointed inference calls
  + [layer_5_intra_forward_pass] is modify attention masks, hidden activations, cache state, cross-attention, or architecture during one model forward pass
  + [layer_6_post_inference] is verify claims, proof, constraints, effects, omissions, and frame fidelity, then accept, revise, reframe, reset, yield, or stop
  = conclusion: inference-time neural tooling is one useful layer, not the complete or universally optimal location of Cantor

& [SharedInferenceBoundary] is the distinction between coordinated inference and literal shared model state
  + [coordinated_shared_state] is an external immutable frame, signed SOP bundle, addressed deliberation ledger, cursor, proof graph, and checkpoint lineage observed by multiple jobs
  + [shared_prefix_optimization] is optional reuse of an identical provider-compatible prefix or cache before jobs fork into private continuations
  + [private_model_state] is each job's hidden activations, sampling state, and continuation cache after divergence
  + [literal_shared_attention] is two or more model jobs jointly reading or mutating one semantically defined hidden attention state during a forward pass
  = must: implement shared inference first as coordination through explicit external state
  = must: treat literal hidden-state sharing as a distinct model-runtime and research problem
  - never: merge independent activation or cache tensors and presume the result preserves meaning

& [HybridAuthoritySplit] is the lawful placement of exact, learned, and generative functions
  + [exact_substrate] owns semantic UUIDs, content digests, signatures, versions, typed relations, permissions, constraints, and proof
  + [learned_router] may propose terms, addresses, relations, paths, tools, faculties, missing perspectives, and priorities with scores and abstention
  + [language_model] may interpret the framed subject, generate hypotheses, deliberate perspectives, explain, and propose results
  + [deterministic_gates] verify every authoritative admission and every consequential effect
  = compact: exact graph compiles, neural routing proposes, inference reasons, deterministic review authorizes
  - never: let learned weights become the sole record of truth, identity, permission, signature, or governing condition

& [InferenceTimeNeuralTooling] is the optional learned proposal path active near model execution
  + [candidate_functions] is embedding retrieval, graph-aware routing, cross-encoder reranking, tool selection, faculty selection, relevance calibration, and safe abstention
  + [input] is purpose, subject, contextual anchors, frame state, uncertainty, and bounded history
  + [output] is scored non-authoritative candidates with model and fabric digests
  + [admission] is exact resolution followed by trust, context, purpose, relation, constraint, budget, and proof gates
  = may: reduce search work and surface non-literal associations
  = must: be compared against exact retrieval and static-frame baselines
  - never: be called optimal without measured task-specific evidence

& [DirectActivationResearchTrack] is the model-internal attention-engineering branch
  + [soft_prompt_or_prefix] is a learned continuous input that later tokens attend to without rewriting the whole base model
  + [adapter_or_fine_tune] is a parameter update teaching recurring Cantor behaviors such as tool timing, structured output, or directive adherence
  + [activation_steering] is a forward-pass change to internal representations intended to bias a model property
  + [runtime_modification] is provider-specific access to masks, caches, layers, hidden states, or cross-model channels
  = may: test whether a compiled attention program improves bounded measured behavior
  = must: declare provider, model, layer, intervention artifact, expected property, off-target tests, and rollback
  = must: remain subordinate to exact semantic evidence and post-inference verification
  - absent: implementation authority before deterministic Cantor baselines and evaluation fixtures exist

& [LearningSequence] is how Cantor earns neural components
  + [step_1] is implement exact signed compilation, lookup, typed graph traversal, and proof
  + [step_2] is implement pre-inference framing, checkpointed queries, and post-inference review
  + [step_3] is log reviewed misses, correct addresses, paths, tool calls, faculty selections, faults, and outcomes
  + [step_4] is define one bounded learned task with labels, negatives, metrics, and deterministic fallback
  + [step_5] is train and evaluate a replaceable router or reranker against non-neural baselines
  + [step_6] is consider learned prefixes, adapters, activation steering, or engine modification only when the external hybrid cannot meet a measured requirement
  = rationale: meaningful weights require stable target identities and reviewed outcome data

& [ComparativeEvaluationGate] is the evidence required to select an intervention layer
  + [condition_A] is model with ordinary task prompt
  + [condition_B] is model with static signed SOP frame
  + [condition_C] is model with exact Cantor retrieval, framing, and proof
  + [condition_D] is condition_C with learned routing or reranking
  + [condition_E] is a model-internal intervention only after D exposes a remaining measured limit
  + [metric_set] is required-address recall, wrong-context rate, unsupported-claim rate, constraint violations, review corrections, latency, tokens, cost, replayability, and proof completeness
  = must: prefer the least invasive layer that satisfies the signed requirement
  = must: retain ablation evidence showing which layer caused an improvement

& [RequirementSet] is the traceable attention-engineering collection
  + [AEI-001] is distinguish external attention architecture from direct transformer-attention control
  + [AEI-002] is place Cantor interventions across compile, adaptation, pre-inference, inter-pass, intra-forward-pass, and post-inference layers
  + [AEI-003] is define shared inference first through explicit external state rather than presumed shared hidden state
  + [AEI-004] is use learned inference tooling for candidate routing and ranking while exact gates retain authority
  + [AEI-005] is train only against stable semantic identities and reviewed outcomes
  + [AEI-006] is evaluate every neural or internal intervention against less invasive baselines
  + [AEI-007] is preserve provider independence until a measured requirement justifies provider-specific internal modification

& [SJSProcessingTrace] is the intervention-layer justification record
  + [fold_1_subject] is whether shared inference, tools, shared attention, and neural-net inference tooling are the correct extension from prompt engineering
  + [fold_2_boundary] is external context and orchestration inside the present Cantor architecture; literal hidden-state sharing and activation intervention in a separate research track
  + [fold_3_data_design] is signed source, semantic address, typed relation, frame, checkpoint, ledger, neural candidate, gate decision, proof, metric, and intervention artifact
  + [fold_4_governance] is exact authority, model-specific disclosure, deterministic fallback, comparative evaluation, safe abstention, and rollback
  + [fold_5_solution_shape] is compile, frame, infer, query, deliberate, verify, learn from reviewed traces, and escalate intervention only when measured
  + [fold_6_fault_review] is attention-weight overclaim, hidden-state conflation, neural authority leakage, premature training, provider lock-in, semantic cache merging, and unmeasured optimality
  + [fold_7_phase_lock] is the staged architecture is formed while implementation remains frozen at the deterministic comparative baseline

